@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task, task id, brief, child run, result, domain event, child port,
  child run state, enqueue time, start time, child driver, transcript, attach command,
  launch spec,
}
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, project root, load error, tool list,
  unresolved root error, model endpoint, parallel limit, general agent,
  model tier, tier error, agent file, timeout, agents map, fallback agent,
  working directory, medium tier,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, task status, parent session id, start-up sweep, task index,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import {
  driver registry, parent adapter, supported child host, child host, parent host,
  parent model,
}

component DelegationService {
  goal {
    Give every *front end* one entry that is ready to delegate: it turns a
    named
    agent, a model tier or neither, and a brief into a task, finds a live task
    by its task id later, and starts clean after an earlier process has died.
  }

  state {
    A *delegation* is the request of a front end. It holds an optional agent
    name, an optional model tier, a *brief text*, optional files, an optional
    expected output, an optional *reply budget* and an optional
    parent session id.
    The reply budget is the number of characters that the child's final reply
    should stay under. Only a front end that knows how its parent host receives
    a
    result gives one, and DelegationService holds no size of its own.

    The *live task registry* holds the tasks that this process has started, by
    their task id and by the parent session id of their delegation.

    A *session cancel* cancels every Pending and live task of one
    parent session id in the live task registry, and reports their task ids and
    how many there were.

    A *collect tool* is the tool of a front end that returns the result of a
    task that its caller did not wait for.

    A *completion notice* tells a parent model that one of its tasks settled:
    it
    names
    the task by its task id with its status, tells the parent model to collect
    the
    result through the collect tool that the front end names, lists the tasks
    of
    the same parent session id that are still Pending or live with their
    status line, and tells the parent model to wait for them before it uses the
    results
    together. It carries no result text, and it names no running tasks and
    gives
    no instruction to wait when none are running. A *stop notice* is the text
    for
    one task that was stopped, through a session cancel or a cancel, and it
    says that the task was stopped and that no action is needed. A
    session cancel
    gives one stop notice for each task it stopped.

    A *queued task* is a task whose child run is Pending in this process, or a
    task
    of another process whose task status is running and that has no start time.
    Queued is a view that DelegationService derives on each reply from the live
    task and the task record, and it is never a stored task status.

    The *queue place* of a child run whose child run state is Pending is its
    position, counted from 1, among the Pending child runs of its
    model endpoint in this process, in the order they arrived.

    A *status line* reports a task that has no result yet. For a queued task of
    this process it gives the model endpoint, the queue place and the time
    waited since the enqueue time, and for a queued task of another process it
    gives the model endpoint and the time waited with no queue place:
    ```text
    queued, not launched yet: no child is running; it starts when endpoint <e>
    has room (place <n> in this session's queue, waiting <elapsed>)
    ```
    For a task whose child run has a start time the status line gives that
    start time and how long ago it was:
    ```text
    running, started <iso> (<elapsed> ago)
    ```

    The *result text* of a settled task starts with a line that holds the
    task id
    first, then the agent, the task status and the failure kind when there is
    one. Lines follow for the message when there is one, the summary, with
    (empty) for an empty summary, and the usage with the duration when
    the result holds usage. A notice goes on the line after the first one, so
    the task id still comes first.
  }

  logic {
    On start-up, ask AgentConfig for the project root from the roots or the
    fallback working directory that the front end gives. When no
    project root resolves, the start-up waits, and each later call resolves it
    again and returns the unresolved root error to its caller, with no task
    made, until one resolves. A front end may ask for the project root to be
    resolved from the roots it reports alone, as a parent host does that
    reports
    one working directory as its only root. Once a project root resolves,
    keep it for
    the service, run the start-up sweep with it, and connect TaskCore to the
    task record, given that project root. The start-up ends when the sweep has
    finished and TaskCore is connected.

    On a delegation, load each agent entry again, giving AgentConfig each
    supported child host that the driver registry lists. When the load fails,
    return the load error to the caller and end the flow with no task made.
    First
    choose the agent. When the delegation names no agent or names the agent
    general, take the general agent. When it names another agent, look up that
    name in the agents map. A name that finds the fallback agent in that lookup
    runs it and reports nothing about a name not configured. When the name is
    unknown, take the fallback agent and report in the reply that the named
    agent
    is not configured. Then choose the model tier: when the delegation names
    one,
    drop a leading tier: from it, and ask AgentConfig for the agent definition
    of
    the chosen agent at that model tier, or else at the agent's own tier or the
    medium tier. When AgentConfig returns the tier error for that agent, return
    it
    to the caller and end the flow with no task made. The task records its
    agent as
    the name of the chosen agent, followed by :<tier> when the delegation names
    a
    model tier.

    With the agent definition found, ask the driver registry for the child port
    of
    its child host, and when no child driver is registered for that child host,
    return the
    error of the driver registry to the caller and end the flow with no task
    made.
    A driver registry that lists no supported child host gives AgentConfig an
    empty
    list
    of supported child hosts, so a request that resolves to any tier gives the
    tier error that names the child host and lists no supported child host,
    before
    the
    driver registry is asked, and no task is made.
    Otherwise render the files, the expected output and the reply budget after
    the brief text into one brief, start the task in TaskCore with that
    agent definition, that brief, that child port and the parent session id,
    and add the task to the live task registry, which a later collect or
    cancel reads. When the child runs of its model endpoint in this process
    that are Starting or Live have reached its parallel limit, keep the
    child run Pending at the next queue place and release it, first come first
    served, when one of them ends, and otherwise release it at once. When the
    caller waits, offer each domain event to the caller and return the result
    when the child run settles, which ends the flow. When the caller does not
    wait, return the task id at once with the start time of a released
    child run, or with the model endpoint, the queue place and the
    enqueue time of a Pending one, which ends the flow.

    Before a collect, a cancel or a listing, run the start-up sweep again, so
    that a task whose owner process died after start-up is not shown as
    running. On a collect or a cancel by task id, look in the
    live task registry and then in the task record, which gives the stored
    result of a settled task or the task status of an abandoned one. A task
    with a stored result gives that result, and a task whose task status is
    abandoned gives no result and says that it is abandoned, which ends the
    flow. A collect of a task in the live task registry that has not settled
    gives no result and returns its start time, or its model endpoint,
    queue place and enqueue time while it is Pending, which ends the flow. A
    cancel of a task that has already settled returns its stored result
    unchanged, which ends the flow. A cancel of a Pending child run removes it
    from the queue of its model endpoint, and each later Pending child run
    moves up one queue place, which ends the flow. A collect of a task whose
    task status is running and that is not in the live task registry gives no
    result, returns its model endpoint, its enqueue time and its start time
    when one is recorded, read from the task record, and says that the task
    belongs to another process, which ends the flow. A cancel of such a task
    reports that it belongs to another process and cannot be cancelled here,
    and changes nothing, which ends the flow.

    On a session cancel for a parent session id, cancel each Pending and live
    task of that parent session id in the live task registry the way a cancel
    does, and return their task ids and count, which ends the flow. A
    session cancel with no parent session id cancels nothing and returns that
    the session is unknown, which ends the flow.

    When a task that its caller did not wait for settles, build its
    completion notice from the task id and status of the task, the name of the
    collect tool of the front end, and the status line of each task of
    the same parent session id that is still Pending or live, and offer it to
    the
    front end that made the delegation, which ends that flow. A task that a
    session cancel or a cancel ended gets the stop notice instead.

    On shutdown, cancel every task in the live task registry, wait up to a
    deadline for their child runs to end, and record each task whose child run
    was then still running as cancelled, so that no task record stays running
    while its owner process lives on, which ends the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    DelegationService must create no task or task record when no child driver
    is
    registered for the child host of the agent definition, when AgentConfig
    returns
    the tier error, or when any agent entry fails to load, and it must load
    each agent entry again on each delegation.

    A delegation that names an agent other than general must find that agent in
    the
    agents map and run it at the model tier that the delegation names, else at
    its
    own tier or the medium tier, except that a name missing from the agents map
    runs
    the fallback agent, and its replies report it queued or started like any
    other
    delegation.

    The brief that the service renders must start with the brief text
    unchanged,
    followed by a Files section, an Expected output section and a Reply size
    section, each only when it is present. The Reply size section asks the
    child
    to keep its final reply under the reply budget and says that the reply is
    returned to the parent model whole and never cut. A delegation with an
    empty
    brief text must be refused whatever its files hold, and the paths of its
    files are not checked.

    Every start-up of a front end, whether a parent adapter or a command,
    must go through
    the start-up of the service, so that the start-up sweep runs at every
    start-up.

    The project root must be resolved once successfully for each DelegationService,
    and the
    agent file, the task record and the child run must all use that same
    project root, which must not change once it is resolved. A process holds at
    most one DelegationService for each parent session at a time. A
    parent adapter
    that builds one DelegationService for each parent session shuts the
    old one down before it starts the next, and a parent adapter that keeps one
    DelegationService for its process ends a parent session with a
    session cancel.
    No other component
    may call for a project root for its own use, and each receives it from
    DelegationService, with one exception: a parent adapter that runs in a
    process
    where no DelegationService starts reads the agent file under the
    project root
    that its parent host reports, because it has no DelegationService to ask. A
    call
    made before the
    project root is resolved must return the unresolved root error and must
    create no task, task record or line of the task index.

    The live task registry must hold no state beyond this process, and the
    task record stays the only durable state of a task.

    A session cancel must reach only the tasks of this process, and a task of
    another process stays as it is.

    A completion notice and a stop notice must never carry the transcript of a
    child run, and the stop notice must never ask the parent model to act.

    A parallel limit must be counted from each child run of this process that
    is Starting or Live, and a child run that is Pending must not be counted.
    A child run that ends in any way, whether Settled, Failed or Cancelled,
    must stop being counted at once and release the next Pending child run. A
    delegation from a command line process or from another session is not
    counted, and nothing may signal or lock across processes to enforce it.

    A child run that is Pending must be returned with its queue place and its
    enqueue time and never with a start time, and the time it waits must not
    count toward its timeout.

    DelegationService depends on TaskCore for the task, on the driver registry
    of
    Kernel for the child port of the child host of an agent definition, on
    AgentConfig
    for the agent definition, the general agent, the fallback agent, the
    tier error
    and the project root, and on TaskStore for the task record, the
    task status and the start-up sweep. It must not depend on any particular
    child driver or parent adapter, and it names no child host, because more of
    both will
    come and
    each plugs in through Kernel.
  }

  cases {
    A delegation with an unknown agent name runs the fallback agent, records
    its
    agent as fallback, and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured. A
    delegation that names the agent general runs the general agent on the
    medium tier and records its agent as general.

    A delegation with an unknown agent name, in an agent file with a
    fallback agent, runs the fallback agent with its own tool list, records its
    agent as fallback, and its reply says that the named agent is not
    configured. A
    delegation that names the agent fallback runs the same agent and records
    the
    same agent, and its reply does not say that a name is not configured.

    A call made before any project root is known returns the
    unresolved root error and writes nothing, and the first call after a
    project root becomes known runs the start-up sweep.

    A delegation while the agent file is malformed returns the load error and
    writes no task record.

    Two delegations to agents on one model endpoint whose parallel limit is 1
    start two child runs. The second stays Pending until the first ends,
    whether it settles, fails by timeout or is cancelled, and is then released,
    and a cancel of the second while it is Pending starts no child.

    Three delegations that do not wait, on a model endpoint whose
    parallel limit is 1, return the first with its start time and the second
    and third Pending at queue place 1 and 2. Cancelling the second moves the
    third to queue place 1.

    A delegation with the model tier medium starts the general agent on the
    child host,
    model and thinking level of that model tier and records the
    agent definition
    general:medium, and tier:medium gives the same task. A delegation with no
    agent
    and no model tier runs the general agent with its full tool list and
    records
    its agent as general, and a delegation that names the agent general and no
    model tier does the same.

    A delegation with a model tier that is not configured returns the
    tier error and
    writes no task record. A delegation that names the agent reviewer and the
    model tier large runs reviewer on large, with reviewer's tier override for
    large
    when
    it has one, and records reviewer:large, and a delegation that names
    reviewer and
    no model tier runs it on its own tier and records reviewer.

    A delegation with files and an expected output gives a brief that starts
    with
    the brief text, then lists the files and the expected output.

    A delegation with a reply budget of 48,000 characters gives a brief that
    ends with a Reply size section that names 48,000 characters, and a
    delegation with no reply budget gives a brief with no Reply size section.

    A collect of a task whose owner process died after the process started says
    that the task is abandoned and has no result, because the sweep ran again
    before the collect.

    A collect by task id after the process restarted returns the stored result
    of a
    settled task, and says that an abandoned task has no result.

    A cancel of a running task that another process owns reports that it
    belongs
    to another process, signals no process and leaves the task status running.

    With two live tasks and one Pending task of parent session id A and one
    live
    task of parent session id B, a session cancel for A cancels the three tasks
    of
    A, reports their three task ids, and leaves the task of B running. A
    session cancel with no parent session id cancels nothing and says that the
    session is unknown.

    With three tasks of one parent session id that the caller did not wait for,
    the first to settle gives a completion notice that names it and its status,
    says how to
    collect its result, lists the other two with their status line and
    gives the instruction to wait for them. A task
    ended by a session cancel gives the stop notice and no completion notice.

    A delegation in a project with no agent file returns the load error that
    says the
    agent file is missing and makes no task, and says to run subzero init.

    A shutdown while a child run ignores its cancel past the deadline records
    that task as cancelled, and its task record does not stay running.
  }

  interface {
    Accept a delegation with an optional agent name, an optional model tier and
    an optional reply budget, which the brief tells the child, refuse it when
    its brief text is empty, and return the result with the task id first once
    the child run settles, or return the task id at once when the caller does
    not wait, with the start time of a released child run,
    or the model endpoint, the queue place and the enqueue time of a Pending
    one. When the named agent is not configured and the fallback agent runs
    instead, return the report that the named agent is not configured with
    the task id, whether or not the caller waits.

    Report each queued task by its model endpoint, by its queue place when it
    is a
    task of this process, and by the time it has waited, and never as started.
    Give every front end the same wording for it, the status line of a task
    that
    has no result and the result text of a settled task, so that no front end
    words the state of a task for itself.

    Offer each domain event of the child run of a waited delegation to the
    caller
    as it happens.

    Hand the project root, once resolved, to the front end that started the
    service, for that front end's own use, and never resolve a different one.

    Let a caller collect the result of a task by task id, returning no result
    while its task status is running, but its start time, or its
    model endpoint,
    queue place and enqueue time while it is Pending, and let a caller cancel a
    task by task id.

    Let a caller wait up to a bound for a task by task id, and answer as a
    collect
    does when the bound passes with the task still unsettled or queued.

    Give a front end each supported child host that the driver registry lists,
    so
    that
    it can describe each routable tier without being given a child driver.

    Give a front end the attach command of a settled task from its task record
    and the child driver registered for its child host. Refuse the attach
    command while the task is running or when its task record has no launch spec.

    Keep a delegation Pending while its model endpoint has reached its
    parallel limit, and release it first come first served.

    List the tasks of one parent session id, or of every parent session id,
    each with its task id, its agent, its task status, the time it was
    created, its start time when one is recorded and its queue place while it
    is Pending in this process, after running the start-up sweep again.

    Offer a session cancel for one parent session id, and list the tasks of one
    parent session id that are still Pending or live.

    Offer the completion notice of each settled task that its caller did not
    wait
    for, with the collect tool of that front end named in it, and the
    stop notice
    of a task that was stopped, to the front end that made its delegation,
    so
    that every parent adapter words them the same
    way and none keeps its own list of running tasks.

    Shut down by cancelling every task in the live task registry and recording
    each one that outlives the deadline as cancelled.
  }

  decisions {
    A separate service keeps TaskCore to the lifecycle of one task, and gives
    the
    start-up sweep, the agent lookup and the live task registry one owner
    instead
    of one for each front end. The files and the expected output are rendered
    into
    the brief text because the child port carries one brief, and the fields
    exist
    to push a parent model toward a fuller brief. The reply budget is a field
    of
    the
    delegation and not a size of the service, because the size a parent model
    can
    take inline is known only to the front end that returns the result.

    A task that runs the fallback agent records its agent as fallback and not
    as
    general, because its tool list is its own and not that of the
    general agent. A
    task that runs the general agent without a model tier records its agent as
    general, whether the delegation names it or names nothing. A tiered
    task records
    its agent as <agent>:<tier>, so that listings, launch names and the frozen
    agent definition show which model tier ran, and the frozen definition with its
    model is
    the proof that the work was routed. The queue place is counted per process
    because the parallel limit is, so it names the place in this session's
    queue
    only.

    The status line and the result text live here, and not in a front end,
    because
    this component is the one owner of the state of a task: the wording of that
    state is shared by every front end, and a front end that imported it from
    another front end would depend on a parent adapter, which the Kernel
    forbids
    of every core service, the command line included. The queued view is
    derived here, so its wording sits beside it. Fitting a reply to the limit
    of
    its parent host and ordering a listing stay with the front end that needs
    them.
  }
}
